# Taste engine

Final numeric taste scores are computed in this folder. Do not generate a dish vector with an LLM.

- Combine with `combineRecipeTaste` / `attributeRecipeTaste`: recipe-relative loudness (smooth blend from `score × (score/peak)^⅓` to full score as max(score/peak, score/avg) → 1; no 85% cliff), smooth blend of linear and p-norm (p=4; each ingredient’s share is weighted by loudness smoothstep ~4→7, then punch weight ramps with that share, midpoint ≈1.5%), linear gain (1.75×). All six dimensions use the same path. Cap each dimension at the max in-ingredient. Contribution tips use the same mix mass (every positive contributor, rounded to 2 decimals with a 0.01 floor; UI shows 5 then “Show more”).
- Unknown ingredients: cache → chemistry leaf (FAO/INFOODS first, then UmamiDB, Phenol-Explorer, Dr. Duke, FooDB, USDA) → Gemini `estimateLeafTaste` if no lab match for that exact name (`source: "llm"`) → nested full recipe search. One Gemini `confirmFoodShortlists` call must accept each source title against the dish context. Match the food, not a keyword collision. A branded USDA product is allowed only when it *is* that ingredient. Juice/paste/oil inherit the flavoring food, not the carrier. UmamiDB wins free glutamate/nucleotides; Phenol-Explorer wins polyphenol bitter; Dr. Duke wins pungents (ppm midpoint). FAO then USDA then FooDB fill remaining classes. Ignore potassium, vitamin C, and protein amino acids. Nutrient sodium is a salty proxy, not culinary salt — Gemini may lower salty when a mouthful is not salt-tasting. Acid-process foods (fermented/pickled/yogurt/vinegar-class) need organic-acid evidence; sodium-only rows are incomplete and fall through to estimate. Do not collapse names (thai chili ≠ chili; soft shell crab ≠ crab). Catalog 0–10 is a mouthful. Tiny compound amounts stay tiny scores. Gemini may calibrate dimensions that have evidence, may add sour/umami when labs omit acids/free glutamate, and may add chili heat when the named food is a chili (USDA often matches sweet/bell pepper). Leaf calibrate/estimate see the parent dish. Do not invent chili heat for ginger, garlic, mustard, or Sichuan peppercorn (0 spicy). Lemon/lime fruit ≈ 9 sour, juice ≈ 9.5; lactic-fermented vegetables ≈ 7–8 sour; orange ≈ 7–8 sweet, onion ≈ 0–1 sweet, black pepper ≈ 0.2 spicy (freshly cracked ≈ 0.5), parmesan ≈ 7–8 salty, soy/fish sauce ≈ 8.5–10 salty; bland vegetables stay near 0. A 10 is the most intense culinary form (salt, sugar, Thai chili). Juice/paste/extract score higher than the whole food. Spicy is chili heat (Thai chili = 10). Chili in a spicy dish is the hot pepper, not sweet chili sauce.
- Representative recipe: every `in` ingredient; volume = mean share across recipes (0 if absent) so occurrence dilutes rare/substitute lines. Extract tags `in` vs `out`; only `in` scores. `mix.intensity` is contribution to the final dish; 0 (drained frying oil, pasta water, evaporated/absorbed cooking water) is dropped from tasting volume; eaten oil stays 1. Non-default intensity also gets `mix.why` (1–2 words); the ingredient hover shows `contributes: N% · why` only then. Out-only sides get an “Often served with” list under the scores (primary flavors), still listed quieter in Ingredients.
- Search recipes in the origin culture’s language by default for the form `identifyDish` resolved (bare name → popular restaurant/tourist form people usually mean; specific qualifier → that variant). Typed-language mode searches in the user’s query language instead; both modes share one dish cache row.
- A recipe counts only if the live page fetch returned 2xx. Accept a recipe only when the live URL and/or fetched HTML title (`og:title` / `<title>` / `h1`) match the dish; LLM extract titles veto other dishes but must not authorize. Captcha/empty shells may fall back to the search-hit title. Multi-word Latin names need a contiguous phrase. URL Context may parse a JS shell after a 2xx fetch; 4xx/5xx pages are skipped. Captcha/human-check redirects still count as 2xx; URL Context and stored links use the pre-challenge recipe URL, not the challenge page.
- Shared dish profiles live in Turso `dishes` (running mean, `timesTasted`, last snapshot). LLM matches the query to a row; it does not invent scores. Nested recipe tastes persist as ingredients, not dishes.
- Site-wide taste count lives in Turso `stats` (`taste_count`). Increment only on a successful top-level profile, not on reject/error/Stop.
- Translate extracted ingredient names to English (or a well-known romanized native name when English is awkward) before listing or resolving. Gemini canonicalizes every name against the ingredient catalog (`canonicalizeIngredientNames(names, catalog, culinaryContext)`). Ambiguous generics must become the cuisine-typical grocery even when the catalog only has the dictionary form (Chinese dish + sausage / 香肠 → chinese sausage or lap cheong, not plain sausage); already-specific names stay themselves.
- Classify the query first (dish / ingredient / reject). If the name is both grocery and dish (spaghetti), classify as dish and skip the ingredient catalog shortcut. Ingredient Turso wins when classify says ingredient. Reuse cache only gates top-level dishes. Brands and gibberish are rejected.
- Resolve every unique listed ingredient before scoring the representative mix.
- Default model: `gemini-3.5-flash-lite`. Hard cases: `gemini-3.6-flash`. IDs in `lib/engine/models.ts`.
- Add measured profiles in Turso (`INSERT OR IGNORE` on normalized name). Existing catalog rows are never overwritten. Unit tests may use `lib/engine/testdata/ingredients.json`; that file is not the live catalog.
- Tests first: `lib/engine/*.test.ts`.
